WEBVTT

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:35.520
welcome back. I'm glad you could join me today. And today I have a special treat for you. I have

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.450
a lovely lady that comes to us out of Fort Lauderdale, Florida, and she's going to do a

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.670
fantastic painting for you. I'd like to introduce you today to Audrey Golden, a long-time friend,

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.250
and I think you'll enjoy watching her as much as I have. So Audrey, we'll turn the show over to you,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.810
and I'll see you when the show's over.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:01:00.000
Okay, thanks, Bob. Hi, it's good to see you. The first thing I'm going to do is I'm going to put

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:08.800
some Magic White on the canvas and cover it three-quarters of the way and leave the bottom

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.520
blank.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:18.330
And as I progress through the painting, you'll understand why I'm not putting Magic White

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.040
at the bottom of the canvas.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:28.970
While I'm doing this, you'll see the colors that I'll use in this painting go across your

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:29.700
screen.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:45.400
You paint with the magic white, make sure you cover your entire surface with an even coat.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:51.600
Come down and just stop about 3/4 of the way down.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:13.220
All right, I've got a new, got a good coat of the Magic Light on the canvas.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:20.820
And now we'll start working on the top part.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:29.640
I'm going to take a two-inch, two-and-a-half-inch brush, and I'm going to load it up.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:39.000
with my alizarin crimson. Be careful because this is, we just want a light coat of pink

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:46.550
through the middle and you'll notice that the alizarin crimson, when it mixes with the

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:52.210
magic white, produces a real pretty pink color.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:03:00.000
I'm doing a Florida sunrise. One day I woke up early in the morning, took my

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.870
camera and went out and watched the sunrise on the beach. So that's the type of picture

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:18.220
where I'll be painting for you. Just feather the pink on up into the sky. I think my horizon

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:24.710
will be about there. I want a little bit of reflection. So I'm going to pull a little

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:30.000
pink down in here. And you'll notice when you don't have a little pink, you'll notice

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:43.220
have the magic white on it won't move for you cover this all up when you do

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.880
water make sure now this is the beginning of water you don't know that

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.670
yet but it is I know it is make sure your brush strokes are nice and

00:03:52.670 --> 00:04:00.000
straight now from there I'm going to take some Indian yellow I have

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.400
I haven't washed my brush or cleaned my brush

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.930
because I can blend this Indian yellow

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.990
and Alizarin crimson together

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:21.670
and get some pretty colors in there.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:25.210
As I'm painting this, I have to decide

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.800
where I'm going to have my sun come up.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.000
And I think it's gonna come right about there.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.950
I want to put a little bit more Indian yellow in that spot.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:45.430
Still have some pretty colors there.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.610
And from that I just want to work a little pathway down.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.170
This will be the reflection of my thumb.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.400
To use your imagination right now,

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.540
but you'll see in a minute what I'm trying to accomplish.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:19.740
You're going to take a clean 2 1/2-inch brush,

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.480
and then just do some very soft blending.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.000
Let the end in.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:58.270
the yellow fade away. Get in here, blend it out, right? Now, I'm going to mix a pretty

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.000
lavender color. I'm going to take

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:06.310
some white, move it down here.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:11.950
Take a little bit of blizzard and crimson,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.740
some Prussian blue.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.260
Watch the Prussian blue because it's strong.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:21.500
Take too much, what happens, it turns blue.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:28.880
Okay, gotta go back and get a little bit more white.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.000
A little more crimson.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:43.610
don't have to mix too well. I'm going to get a little bit of Van Dyke in here too

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:53.100
just to soften the color. You can leave it marbleized. It doesn't have to be mixed to death.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:07:00.000
And then I'm going to take a one-inch brush and start just working some

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.930
coloring in here and through the sky.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:07.590
Because on that particular morning,

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:14.180
I saw a lot of purple.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.740
And then in spots, I even saw some gray.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.840
Just feather those clouds in.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:39.180
Now one thing you have to be careful about is that you have to be careful that your Indian

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:44.430
Yellow and your Lavender work together.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:52.880
If you put too much Indian Yellow around here or at the outside, you might get too, you'll

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.830
get a green color if you're not careful, so just keep it on the edges.

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:04.360
Just wiggle a little old cloud in through here.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:15.260
And then again, I take my blending brush and soften everything.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:26.080
Using the Magic White, such a treat because you can really blend super, super colors.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:39.280
Right in here just a little lavender and you'll see all sorts of neat colors appear.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:57.140
We have some beautiful sunrises and beautiful sunsets in Florida.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:06.850
and the colors in the sky sometimes you wouldn't even believe what you see if you painted it

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:16.210
people would say now that can't happen but it does all right now i want to put a little bit

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:29.410
of van dyke brown corners and it's going to look more of a gray color i just want to

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:41.040
up my corners a little bit. Again, the spandegle. Just work in and give it a real soft look.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:10:00.000
Wiggle. And as you're working on either a sunrise or a sunset or any

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:16.480
You can put lots of different colors in and just keep working them to see the different effects you get.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:27.090
And again, just blend that and soften it.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.250
Don't be afraid to blend.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:40.980
Even if the lavender and the yellow go together,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.020
even if you get a little tint of green in there,

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.580
it really doesn't hurt that much

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:49.530
because I've seen skies where that happens.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:52.130
It looks very natural.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:56.730
All right, now let me show you

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.120
where my horizon's going to go.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.960
I'm going to take my fan brush and put some of my lavender color on.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:12.270
And I want to, I've kind of left an opening here where I want to create my horizon.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:18.460
I just want to draw a soft line.

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:30.000
When you do a horizon, make sure you have a straight line.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:35.840
I've got it. It's just going to tell me where I'm going.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:39.390
And then, taking my fan brush,

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:49.410
and I just want to make little lines across.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:54.110
Now the pink from my sky is reflecting into my water,

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:59.800
and when you do this, don't cover up the pink.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:13.730
that's going to be the sky reflecting. Keep your lines straight, and on this day the water

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:24.940
was very calm, so there weren't many. There were some ripples, but there weren't very

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:29.570
big waves. So I just want some real soft lines in there.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:42.890
too. What you can do is you can just take your blending brush, blend it, and you get

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:49.590
all sorts of wonderful colors there. Same thing with the other side. I put my lines

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:57.300
on with the tip of the brush. If you want to, you can go like that, but you have more

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.440
control over doing it this way.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:13.640
and come in to where your reflection of the sun will be.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:17.550
That still looks pretty abstract, doesn't it?

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:21.120
But you'll see as we go on what's going to happen.

00:13:45.390 --> 00:14:00.000
dark shadows in here just again straight lines some a little bit of Van Dyke a little bit blue

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:11.590
bit of crimson all mixed together to give me a darker lavender color or purple and just a little

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:28.340
few lines across here again i want my brightest part to be right in the middle all right now

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.000
the next thing i have to do

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:42.040
I have to put my sun in. Take a little bit of white on my fan brush. I'm just going to

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:45.880
slide, look at it, see where my sun should be. It's going to be right about here.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:53.540
Just place it on there and just with the fan brush, pivot the fan brush.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:15:00.000
It'll pick up some of the orange. That's fine. Otherwise, if you if you just make a round white

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.860
dot there it looks like you pasted it on. So you do want it to blend in with your

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:11.850
background. Now if for some reason you get too much paint on there just take your

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:30.000
palette knife, scrape a little bit of it off because what I'm going to do is again I'm going to take

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:45.000
my brush and blend it. Get spots, just move it, just move the paint. Now sometimes when

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.050
I have students doing this, we don't always get a round circle. So what I tell them to

00:15:50.050 --> 00:16:00.000
do is just pull a cloud across it. And that way you don't have to worry about getting

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:11.910
a round circle. Break the edge of the sun up a little bit, especially at sunrise you won't see.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:18.280
The sun keeps popping out from under the clouds. Sunrise.

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:29.410
And then we can put a little bit over here using the fan brush, a few little soft clouds.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:43.190
Just blend them in.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:49.840
Okay, now if you wanted to make this a sunset, I would just put more color into it.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:56.040
You could really get this to be a bright, pretty sky.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.920
But don't be afraid to try different colors in your skies.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:06.400
You don't have to just stick to blue with white clouds.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.940
All right, now I've got my sun in there.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.400
I've got the beginning of some water.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:20.620
I'm going to take my knife, take some paint, white.

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:26.720
Just going to start back here.

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:29.090
Get a little ridge of that paint on there.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:32.180
I'm going to use some magic white here.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:45.800
Slide down the canvas.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:56.310
As I come forward, lines are going to get bigger.

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:15.140
slip and slide across, if you want to, like what you've done or you don't,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:21.410
I just take a little fan brush and smooth out my edges, my water lines, or my reflection.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:44.970
we go. So we've got some water, we've got the sun, and we've got the sky. And the

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:52.450
next thing I'm going to do is I'm going to take some magic black and my one-inch

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:56.400
brush. I'm going to start filling in down here.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:09.700
This is where I left the canvas dry, and if you wanted to, you could use your 2 1/2 inch

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:17.080
brush to get it finished in a hurry, or put this in.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:30.000
Now I happen to be sitting on a sand dune, so I want to get a little bit of height over

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:36.120
here plus what's going to happen when I get my shadows in there they tell people

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:45.280
that the sand isn't black it's white but it as the Sun came up the sand looked

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:53.170
black because it was creating a silhouette just put this on

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:18.100
Cover the bottom a little bit.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:22.960
And now my fan brush.

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:29.570
And I want some of the color from the

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:37.000
water in my magic white now i that was a clean fan brush but what i'm taking is i'm taking

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:42.740
some of the magic white from where my water was and actually just blending it right down into my

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:51.830
sand and you get a natural highlight from that go up a little bit higher

00:20:51.830 --> 00:21:00.000
because that paint is still wet and that's where the

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:30.000
sun would be shining on the sand and works down. Let's make that a little bit higher.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:59.690
fan brush, just take the side of it and pull up some grass that was growing on the beach.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:23.370
Dribble. Just use the side of the brush. A little bit of color into this. Make a little

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:28.840
path. And I'm just taking the lavender color that I used.

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:40.210
working down and as I come forward my path is going to get a little bit bigger and bigger.

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:52.280
And you can play with this and you can get all sorts of shadows and highlights.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:57.140
You're actually down in this area working dark to light,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:04.450
So just a little bit of color here and there.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:10.650
You can get some little sand.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:20.990
Make the sand actually move for you.

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:26.330
Now we take the liner brush

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:29.800
and dip it into the black.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:37.710
That particular sand dune that I was sitting on were sea oats.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:44.850
I'm going to put a little bit of paint thinner with this just to get it really thin.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:59.280
Roll it around, and on this side, put some little thin liner lines in there,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:18.510
sea oats have little tiny dots on them. They look like a piece of wheat. They're used to

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:27.890
keep the water from blend, to keep the water from eroding the soil, the sand, so they're

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:30.000
not allowed to pick from in Florida.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.890
And this one's going to go way up.

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:54.130
You can do all sorts of things with these to be free.

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:59.090
Go up into the air and then pull a few little dots down.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:19.600
with a few little pieces of grass here.

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:27.970
Also on that morning, there were some pelicans flying by.

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:30.000
And how I do that,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:39.000
is I just take my liner and I tell people to make a V,

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:47.360
fill it in, let them fly by, or an M.

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:51.070
So I'm coming in for a landing.

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:53.200
And then I have another one down here.

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:16.880
I'm going to clean my brush, and just a little lavender as a highlight, some of these, okay?

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:23.900
And then, continue with my lavender and sign my name.

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:30.000
This is a very simple painting.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.210
and I know you'll enjoy doing it.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:36.760
The best part is when we put the black in.

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:40.700
Hi.

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:43.000
There we go.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:44.370
- Isn't she fantastic?

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:45.880
I told you you'd love her.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.300
From all of us here, we'd like to wish you happy painting.

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:50.420
God bless.
